' ===========================================================================
' SERVICE OPERATIONS (0034-0047, and the 20260811-20260812 files)
'
' The fourth section of this diagram, and the first whose principal is not
' already inside a community: a service professional registers themselves, is hired by
' communities afterwards, and goes on existing when every one of them drops
' them. Same three-layer shape -- a router does HTTP and authorization, a
' service holds the rule, a repository is the only thing that speaks to
' Postgres -- with two structural additions:
'
'   1. A THIRD background worker. Dispatcher claims timers out of
'      dispatch_tasks with a LEASE and fires them, at-LEAST-once, which is the
'      opposite of what PushSender chose. A dropped push is a missed buzz; a
'      dropped resident_timeout is a complaint left waiting forever with
'      nobody coming. So every firing function is idempotent instead.
'   2. AUTHORITY IS ASKED OF THE DATABASE, not inferred from a membership
'      role. can_hire_for_department, can_manage_department,
'      can_supervise_department and gate_admin_community_for each answer for
'      ONE department, because the same admin may hire for one and not the
'      next, and because rank and role are separate axes -- a security
'      department's roster manager holds membership_role = security.
'      /admin, /manager, /security-manager and /worker are four SURFACES over
'      these same routers, not four permission models.
' ===========================================================================
